# Copy to .env.local for development. .env* is gitignored, so real values never get committed.
#
# For deploys, set the same two variables in the hosting environment (Netlify:
# Site configuration > Environment variables) rather than committing a file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MongoDB — stores start-a-project submissions from /contact
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The full SRV connection string from Atlas, including the password. Take it from
# Atlas > Cluster > Connect > Drivers and replace <db_password> with the real one.
#
# The password must be percent-encoded if it contains @ : / ? # [ ] or %, because
# those are URI-reserved characters and the driver will misparse the string. An @
# in a password is the usual culprit: encode it as %40.
#
# Do NOT put the database name in the path — MONGODB_DB below sets it, so the same
# cluster can serve staging and production without editing a string that also
# carries credentials.
MONGODB_URI="mongodb+srv://USERNAME:PASSWORD@CLUSTER.xxxxx.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority&appName=Zefract"

# Which database on that cluster to write to. Defaults to `zefract` if unset.
# Point staging at something else (`zefract_staging`) so test submissions never
# land in the list the client actually reads.
MONGODB_DB="zefract"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP — the internal notification a contact submission triggers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WITHOUT SMTP_HOST AND MAIL_TO, NO EMAIL IS SENT. The submission is still stored
# and the visitor still sees the success message, but nobody is told. The server
# log says so explicitly on every submission — look for
# "stored, but NO EMAIL WAS SENT". Do not ship without these set.
#
# SMTP_HOST examples: smtp.gmail.com · smtp.zoho.com · smtp.resend.com ·
# email-smtp.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com (SES)
SMTP_HOST="smtp.example.com"

# 587 with STARTTLS is the usual choice; 465 is implicit TLS. `secure` is derived
# from this (465 => true), so the two cannot get out of step. Override only if a
# provider does something unusual.
SMTP_PORT="587"
# SMTP_SECURE="true"

# Leave SMTP_USER unset for a relay that authenticates by IP instead of password.
SMTP_USER="apikey-or-username"
SMTP_PASS="the-smtp-password"

# The envelope sender. MUST be an address on a domain the provider is allowed to
# send for, or the mail is rejected or binned as spoofed. This is NOT the
# visitor's address — their address goes in Reply-To, so hitting Reply in the
# notification answers them directly.
MAIL_FROM="Zefract website <no-reply@zefract.com>"

# Who receives the notification. Comma-separated for more than one inbox.
MAIL_TO="hello@zefract.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin — the in-site blog admin at /admin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WITHOUT BOTH OF THESE, NOBODY CAN LOG IN. The login action refuses outright when
# either is unset, so a deploy that forgets them fails closed rather than open.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH is a bcrypt hash, never the password itself. Generate it with:
#
#   node scripts/hash-admin-password.mjs "the-password"
#
# which prints both values ready to paste. The password is the whole wall around
# /admin, so make it long; the script refuses anything under 12 characters.
#
# EVERY $ IN THE HASH MUST BE ESCAPED AS \$ IN A .env FILE. A bcrypt hash is full
# of $ characters, and Next's env loader expands $NAME references in values
# whatever the quoting — the hash loads mangled and every login fails with "not
# right". The script prints the line already escaped; paste it whole. Hosting
# dashboards (Netlify's env UI) do no expansion, so THERE the raw hash goes in
# as-is — the script prints that form too.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH="\$2b\$12\$paste-the-generated-hash-here"

# Signs the admin session cookie (HS256). Must be at least 32 characters of real
# randomness — the script above prints a fresh one. ROTATING THIS SIGNS THE ADMIN
# OUT EVERYWHERE, which is also the way to revoke a session on a lost device.
SESSION_SECRET="paste-32-plus-random-characters-here"

# Two-factor. Where the six-digit sign-in code is emailed after the password is
# accepted. SETTING THIS TURNS TWO-FACTOR ON; leaving it unset means the password
# alone signs in, which is the behaviour this shipped with.
#
# IT NEEDS SMTP_HOST ABOVE TO BE WORKING. With an address set but no working mail
# server, a production login FAILS CLOSED — the code cannot be delivered, so the
# attempt is refused rather than waved through, because a second factor that can
# be skipped when convenient is not a second factor. Test the contact form first.
# In development the code is written to the server log instead, so the flow can be
# exercised without a mail server.
#
# A PERSONAL INBOX, NOT MAIL_TO. MAIL_TO is the sales address and may well be
# shared or forwarded to several people; a sign-in credential should land in one
# inbox belonging to the person who owns the password.
# ADMIN_2FA_EMAIL="you@yourdomain.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudinary — images uploaded from the blog admin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WITHOUT ALL THREE, IMAGE UPLOAD IS REFUSED. The editor says so and the server
# log names the missing variables; nothing silently falls back to another store.
# Posts whose covers predate Cloudinary keep rendering from MongoDB either way.
#
# From the Cloudinary dashboard: Settings > API Keys.
#
# THE CLOUD NAME IS PUBLIC AND THAT IS FINE. It appears in every delivery URL,
# so NEXT_PUBLIC_ gives away nothing a page source would not. It is the one
# value the browser needs, because it builds image URLs itself.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME="your-cloud-name"

# THE KEY AND SECRET ARE SERVER-ONLY. Note there is no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and
# there must never be: the secret signs uploads, and anyone holding it can
# upload to and delete from your account. It is read by lib/cloudinary/config.ts,
# which is imported only by server code — importing it from a client component
# is a build error, which is the outcome we want.
#
# Uploads go straight from the browser to Cloudinary using a signature this
# server generates per upload. The file never passes through the site, so the
# 5MB limit is a policy rather than a hosting constraint, and the secret stays
# here. Each signature is good for one upload, into one folder, of one of four
# formats, under 5MB, for one hour.
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY="000000000000000"
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET="paste-the-api-secret-here"
#
# Assets are filed under admin-panel/posts/covers and admin-panel/posts/body.
# An abandoned draft can leave an image nothing references; `npm run media:sweep`
# lists them (and deletes with --delete). It only touches assets older than 24h
# that no post points at, so it cannot remove something being edited right now.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Search Console — site ownership verification
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The content value of the HTML-tag verification method: Search Console > add
# property > Ownership verification > HTML tag,
#
# VERIFY THE APEX, https://zefract.com — that is the canonical host (content/site.ts),
# and it is the one every canonical tag, sitemap entry and JSON-LD URL names. A URL-prefix
# property for the www host would report on a hostname the app redirects away from, so it would
# show almost no traffic. A Domain property covers both and is the better choice where DNS
# access is available.
# then copy ONLY the content="..." value, not the whole tag. When set, the root
# layout renders the <meta name="google-site-verification"> tag; when unset, no
# tag is rendered at all. Needed once per property; harmless to leave set.
# GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION="paste-token-here"
